module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.identity_in_the_comparator_spelling_in_the_renderer

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data identity_in_the_comparator_spelling_in_the_renderer: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "identity_in_the_comparator_spelling_in_the_renderer" as NonEmptyStr,
  receipts: [
    "**one fact is answered by IDENTITY in the comparator and by SPELLING in the renderer, and a later consumer keys on the renderer** (INVALID STATE: two keys for one entity, held by a consumer that was written after the comparator and reasonably assumed the two agreed). The asymmetry IS the class. Nobody wrote a spelling-keyed comparison: the comparator was correct from the start, so every test of the DECISION passes, and the defect lives entirely in a string that existed only to be read by humans until something started keying on it.",
    "HARM. The renderer's output is not stable under edits the comparator is immune to. A consumer keyed on it therefore holds entries that a semantically irrelevant change -- an import rewritten from a bare name to a qualified one -- silently moves off their key. Where that consumer is an ADMISSION roster, the consequence inverts the mechanism: an already-admitted site stops matching its row, takes the refusing arm, and the ratchet fires over a population change nobody made. The failure is not that something is admitted wrongly; it is that a correct admission evaporates.",
    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS. This is NOT a spelling-keyed comparator (the comparison itself was always on identity), and it is NOT an unresolved reference (both spellings resolve, to the same declaration). The tell is an ASYMMETRY between two functions that a reader assumes agree: one resolves before comparing, the other renders what was authored. It is visible only when the same entity is reached from callers that spell it differently -- so a corpus where every caller happens to use one spelling hides it completely, and a narrow instrument reports clean.",
    "RECEIPT (2026-09-22, smart-tern-891, gunbc#12047). v1.compiler.infer resource_requirement_diags rendered the callee's required resource with type_node_label, which is the CALLER'S import spelling, and the resource-requirement frontier roster then keyed its rows on that string. The whole-corpus census measured ONE resource under TWO labels -- `Network` from 28 sites and `std.resources.Network` from 9. established_resource_binding, the comparator that decides whether the requirement is met, was never affected: it matches through resource_declaration_identity, so both spellings resolve to one declaration. Repaired by resource_identity_label, which labels the RESOLVED DECLARATION and is used for both the admitted and the refusing diagnostic; its Absent arm falls back to the written spelling and fabricates nothing, because a resource whose name resolves to no declaration establishes nothing either and that call is already refused.",
    "HOW IT WAS FOUND, which is the transferable part: by MEASURING THE POPULATION, not by reading the key. The key looked right on inspection -- five declaration-identity facts -- and the fork was invisible until the census printed the same resource twice under different names. A reviewer reading the roster type would have agreed it was identity-grained, and so did its author.",
    "POSSIBLE DUPLICATE, STATED RATHER THAN RESOLVED: this may be the same class as gunbc.recurring_failure_mode two_admission_authorities_keyed_on_different_relations, filed by gentle-dove-188 in gunbc#12055, which is NOT on main at this writing. If that row lands, this specimen should be APPENDED to it and this file deleted -- one class, one row. It is filed separately only because a row that names its possible duplicate is cheap to consolidate, while two rows that do not know about each other are the DESIGN section 3 fork this ledger exists to record.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: outside the ladder -- silent wrongness. The forked key produced no diagnostic; it would have surfaced as an admitted site refusing after an unrelated import edit, attributed to whatever change happened to be in flight.",
    "CEILING: structurally impossible. A renderer that answers a question a comparator already answers is a second authority for one relation; the state is unwritable once the name a consumer keys on is DERIVED from the resolved declaration rather than produced beside it.",
    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: an entity reference whose rendered form is a projection of its resolved identity rather than of its authored spelling, so that no consumer can key on a caller-relative name at all -- sufficient that the two-labels-one-entity state has no constructor, rather than being repaired per renderer as each fork is measured.",
  ],
  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.infer", decl_name: "resource_identity_label", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.infer", decl_name: "established_resource_binding", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.infer", decl_name: "resource_requirement_frontier", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
